{"type":"assistant","message":{"model":"claude-opus-5-5","id":"msg_011CfZcsuJguxCZohE5zUYKQ","type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"text","text":"Item 07 is done: Fomin's Theorem 8, `pw(G) ≤ sb(G) ≤ pw(G) + 1`, is proved in Lean with no `sorry`. The gate passes: lake build ok, the one allowed `sorry` is still the one in `Sandwich.lean`, and 1,231 tests pass.\n\n**What's new.** `lean/MOSPFormalization/Complex/SplitBandwidth.lean`, imported from the root. `#print axioms` shows only `propext`, `Classical.choice` and `Quot.sound`.\n- **Definitions, as Fomin §3.2 gives them:**\n  - `IsNodeSplitting`: one vertex becomes two adjacent vertices, and its neighbours are split between them into two groups, either of which may be empty.\n  - `IsSplit`: a copy of `G` followed by any finite number of such splittings.\n  - `splitBandwidth`: the smallest bandwidth over all splits, using `Sandwich.lean`'s bandwidth.\n\n  None of these mentions pathwidth.\n- **Lower half (`pathwidth_le_splitBandwidth`).** Only the special case Fomin's proof needs is proved: a single splitting cannot lower pathwidth (`pathwidth_le_of_isNodeSplitting`). The proof merges the two new vertices back into one in every bag. The general fact that pathwidth never drops when passing to a minor was not needed and is not formalised. The rest follows from `pathwidth_le_bandwidth`.\n- **Upper half (`splitBandwidth_le_pathwidth_add_one`).** This does not follow Fomin's route, which goes through interval bandwidth (his Thm 6). Instead I build a split directly from a vertex ordering taken from item 04's narrowness work:\n  - each vertex is replaced by a path of copies, one for each step at which it is in Kornai & Tuza's shack;\n  - sorted by step, this split has bandwidth at most the ordering's narrowness, which is `pw + 1`;\n  - a separate proof shows the graph really is a split, reached by adding the copies one node splitting at a time.\n- **No hypotheses.** Fomin assumes connected graphs with at least two vertices. Both halves hold here for every finite graph, including the empty one.\n\n**Not proved.** That both ends of the sandwich actually occur, e.g. `sb(K_{1,3}) = 2 = pw + 1`. Proving it needs a lower bound over every split of `K_{1,3}`, so it stays with the brute-force checker. Fomin's Theorems 3 and 6 (`sb = ib`) are not formalised either, and the proof does not use them.\n\n**Records updated:**\n- `paper2/equivalences.md`: row 9 is marked as a sandwich and proved, the §9 status line is updated, and there is a new section \"Item 07\".\n- `PROGRESS.md`: Iteration 7 added and the count set to 7/14.\n- `iterations.md`: item 07 is marked `[x]`.\n\nNothing is committed."}],"container":null,"stop_reason":null,"stop_sequence":null,"stop_details":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":2,"cache_creation_input_tokens":689,"cache_read_input_tokens":184579,"cache_creation":{"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":689,"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":0},"output_tokens":817,"service_tier":"standard","inference_geo":"global"},"input_transformations":[],"diagnostics":null,"context_management":null},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"d39d506d-8faf-4025-af16-ad1feed770f1","uuid":"c987b446-5be2-422d-9604-244342f7da86","timestamp":"2026-09-30T12:25:07.507Z","request_id":"req_011CfZcstqAL2UJ92BaHMCVU"}
